#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""grabd: D-Bus service that records application audio streams.

Bus name org.grab.Recorder, object /org/grab/Recorder, interface org.grab.Recorder1

  ListItems()            -> a(sa{sv})  items in display order (id, properties)
  ListInputs()           -> a(ssb)     microphones: (name, description, is_default)
  Toggle(s id)                          available -> recording -> paused -> recording ...
  SetInput(s id, s input)               "" none, "default" follow default mic, or source name
  Remove(s id, b with_data)             remove a finished item (done / empty / failed);
                                        with_data also moves its files to the Trash
  Delete(s id)                          remove a finished item and delete its files
                                        permanently
  RemoveFinished(b with_data)           Remove() every finished item
  DeleteFinished()                      Delete() every finished item
  SaveNow(s id)                         save an MP3 snapshot of a recording/paused item now
  SetPlaybackMuted(s id, b muted)       stop/resume playing a stream on its device; it is
                                        still recorded (see "Muted playback" below)
  SetEchoCancel(s id, b on)             cancel the speakers' echo in the item's input
                                        (default on; see "Echo cancellation" below)
  Meters(b on)                          stream level meters: on for 5 s after each call
                                        (clients repeat it while they show levels)
  signal Levels(s)                      while meters are on, ~15 times a second: JSON
                                        {item id: level 0..1 (RMS)} for items with a stream
  RemoveRecord(s id)                    discard a recording/paused item's recording: stop it,
                                        move its MP3 and FLAC to the Trash; the stream is
                                        offered again
  DeleteRecord(s id)                    RemoveRecord(), deleting the files permanently
  GetSettings()          -> s          JSON {"folder": recordings folder ("" = default),
                                        "default_folder"}
  SetSettings(s json)                   change some settings (same keys; "folder": "" for
                                        the default); fails with a message if the folder
                                        can't be created or written. Saved in
                                        ~/.config/grabd/settings.json
  signal SettingsChanged(s)             GetSettings() after a change
  signal ItemsChanged(a(sa{sv}))        full item list after any change
  signal InputsChanged(a(ssb))          full input list after any change
  GetState()             -> s          JSON {"items": [{properties}], "inputs":
                                        [{"name", "description", "default"}], "folder"}
  signal StateChanged(s)                GetState() after any change (for clients
                                        that handle JSON more easily than variants)

Item properties: id, title, app, state, busy, pending, muted, echo_cancel, stream, input,
input_description, file, duration, error.
  state:   available | recording | paused | encoding | done | empty | failed
  busy:    an MP3 snapshot/final encode is queued or running for this item
  pending: a Toggle is in progress (starting, pausing or resuming); further
           Toggles are ignored until it is done
  muted:   the stream's playback is muted (SetPlaybackMuted)
  error:   why the last start failed (state stays available), or for
           finished items what went wrong

Recording: one hidden 4-channel virtual null sink per recording; the app stream
is linked (alongside its normal routing) into its first two channels and the
chosen input into the other two. One ffmpeg captures it as a single stream
("Grab: <title>" in volume controls), splits the channels, gates the input's
background noise, mixes both and writes a lossless FLAC with silence removed.
If the input's source goes away or changes (mic unplugged and replugged, new
default), it is relinked. Pause unlinks and saves an MP3
snapshot; resume relinks and appends. When the stream disappears, a new
stream of the same process with the same title within STREAM_GRACE seconds
takes its place (Firefox replaces a stream paused for a few seconds when it
resumes); otherwise the item is finalized (state encoding -> done/empty/failed) and stays until removed.
Encodes are serialized in one background queue and coalesced per item.

Muted playback: the stream is linked into a private silent sink (grab_silent,
so it keeps running) and WirePlumber's link to its playback device is deleted.
The stream's "Play on" metadata is not touched, so WirePlumber saves nothing
and other streams of the same app are unaffected. Unmuting points the stream's
target at the silent sink and then at its device (or the default output), so
WirePlumber links it again, and finally restores the target metadata as it was.

Echo cancellation: the input goes through PipeWire's WebRTC echo canceller
(with noise suppression) before the recording sink, with the monitor of the
default output as the echo reference. It runs in a separate "pipewire -c"
process, one per input/output pair, shared by recordings and stopped when
unused. Its nodes are hidden from pipewire-pulse (Internal media classes, not
autoconnected; grabd links them), so other apps keep the raw microphone and
what you hear is untouched.

Level meters: a hidden capture (pw-record, media.class Stream/Input/Audio/
Internal, so not listed by pipewire-pulse) linked directly to each stream's
outputs, like the recording link: it measures what the stream produces, i.e.
what is recorded, wherever the stream plays (muted, "Play on" moves).

Metadata lives in the files' tags (ID3 in the MP3, Vorbis comments in the
FLAC): title, date (start time), and grab=1 (the marker), grab_app,
grab_input. MP3s in the recordings folder that carry the marker and belong to
no item (earlier sessions) are listed as done items; others are not listed.
Removing a finished item strips the marker (a lossless copy of the MP3) and
keeps the MP3 (and renames a failed one's FLAC to a visible
"<name>.flac"); removing it with data moves the MP3 and any leftover FLAC to
the Trash (restoring them brings the item back); Delete() deletes them. Items disappear when their
MP3 is deleted or loses the marker.

Requires: python-dbus-next, ffmpeg, pipewire-pulse (pactl), pipewire (pw-link, pw-dump).
Optional: python-mutagen (reads tags and removes the marker in place, in
milliseconds; without it ffprobe reads tags and ffmpeg copies the file).
"""
